// [basic.types.general]: a conditional expression whose second and third
// operands are both `void` is itself a `void` expression. Discarding it
// as an expression statement is legal, and this is what that lowers to:
// an ordinary condition/then/else/merge diamond in which each arm is
// generated purely for its effect and the merge block merges nothing.
//
// Codegen used to name the merged result unconditionally --
// `%condtmp = phi void [ <badref>, %cond.then ], [ <badref>, %cond.else ]`
// -- and LLVM's verifier rejects an instruction that has a name but no
// value, so this legal program was reported as
// "internal error: generated invalid IR: Instruction has a name, but
// provides a void value!". movecheck had already decided the opposite,
// and correctly: its Conditional case asks
// check_expression_yields_a_value only of the *condition*, leaving the
// result legal when the whole conditional is discarded.
//
// Any reintroduction of the phi fails this case outright rather than
// merely changing the IR: `generate()` runs LLVM's verifier per function,
// so a `phi void` produces no IR at all and every assertion below fails.
int taken = 0;

void h() { taken = 1; }
void h2() { taken = 2; }

int main() {
    bool c = true;
    c ? h() : h2();
    return 0;
}
